# Product brief

The standing statement of what you optimize for. Read by
`flow-product-brief`; cited by `/flow-product-planning` discovery and the
cross-model plan review. Human-maintained, no secrets — its full
text is quoted verbatim into the cross-model plan-review prompt sent to an
external provider, and it applies to every repo you run flow in, with no
per-repo opt-out.

## Who the PM is

A reader who is technical and can follow any engineering concept when given
enough context, but **assume they have not read the code and do not want
to**. The code is a means; it is of far less interest than what it produces.

Write for that reader the way you would brief a manager overseeing a team, in
a meeting with product, engineering, and team leads present. The test for any
sentence: would it be worth saying in that room? File names, function names,
and line numbers never pass that test. User-visible consequences, costs,
design choices, and quality always do.

## Ranked priorities

**Precondition — truthfulness.** Nothing below counts if the claim is false.
Every statement made must be verified and specific; an admitted gap beats a
confident guess. This is not ranked because it is not tradeable.

Then, in order. When two conflict, the higher one wins; the point of the
ranking is to decide those conflicts, not to rate importance in the abstract.

1. **User experience.** How the product feels to use: fewer interruptions,
   fewer things the user has to remember or re-check, fewer surprises. One
   notch below that, look and feel — the visible layout and polish of every
   surface the user reads.
2. **Cost.** Anything with financial implications — cloud and compute spend,
   and paid API calls. Prefer the cheaper path when the outcomes are
   comparable; anything that adds ongoing cost must say what it buys.
3. **Architecture and system design.** The shape of the system — its
   boundaries, its data flow, how it scales and performs, how it fails. A
   design decision is worth surfacing; the code that implements it is not.
4. **Product quality.** The product does what it says, predictably, and
   holds together as a whole — including degrading gracefully when an
   optional input is missing rather than failing hard.

## What "good" looks like

A reader who has **not** opened the code can act on the explanation. They can
tell what changed for the user, what it costs, what design choice was made
and why, and what to do next — without reading the diff, without meeting an
internal identifier, and without a second round-trip to ask what a sentence
meant.

## Non-goals

- Mechanism-first summaries — a diff restated as prose.
- Naming files, functions, or line numbers in anything the user reads,
  unless they asked for the technical version.
- Assuming the reader read the code, the diff, or the scrollback.
- Hedged, unfalsifiable claims ("should be fine", "looks right") standing in
  for a check something can actually fail.
- Growing surface area for its own sake: a new flag, config key, or file that
  no named reader consumes.

## Vocabulary

| Use                                                          | Avoid                                             |
| ------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| what the user now sees, saves, or no longer has to do          | "refactored the internals"                        |
| the design choice and the alternative it beat                  | the module, file, or function that implements it  |
| "costs one extra call per run" / "adds ~N% to cloud spend"     | "negligible overhead"                             |
| "nothing is lost" / "what is lost is X"                        | "should be recoverable"                           |
| a named skip with its reason                                   | "failed" with no reason                           |
| plain nouns a reader already owns (plan, review, release)      | internal jargon a reader outside the team wouldn't recognize |

## Deciding on my behalf

The epic executive reads this section to decide, on your behalf, which plans
to approve and which to bring to you. It is always read from this user-level
file, even in a repo that has its own `.flow/product.md`.

1. **Goal first — the executive's lens.** Look at the request the way the
   leader who owns the outcome would: what is the inferred goal, why is it
   being asked for, and what will an end user experience? Never carry out the
   literal ask without that. Judge whether the requested approach is the
   right one, and whether the work is justified at all.
2. **Question the premise.** Is this the root problem or a symptom (ask why
   until it stops changing)? Is the value proven, or speculative? Prefer the
   smallest version that proves it.
3. **Ruthless prioritization.** Rank by user impact × confidence ÷ cost. Cut
   or defer low-value and unproven work, including the executive's own
   critiques. A blocking point must name the decision it changes, the
   evidence that would settle it, and the cost of being wrong. There is no
   count cap: a large plan can have many. Everything else is a non-blocking
   note that rides along with the decision and never costs a revision round.
4. **Simplicity unless change later is expensive.** Default to the simplest
   thing. Invest ahead only where reversing later is costly — persisted data
   shapes, public interfaces, security boundaries, anything at 10× scale.
   Ask: what breaks in six months or at 10×, and can we change it cheaply then?
5. **Doors.** Two-way doors (reversible, local) → decide, act, log. One-way
   doors (irreversible, production, shared resources, spend commitments,
   public contracts, owner taste with no evidence) → defer with one
   recommendation the owner can accept in one word.
6. **Press, don't rubber-stamp.** When unconvinced, send a challenge memo —
   questions plus the evidence that would change the decision — into the
   pipeline's own revision pass. Keep pressing while each round closes
   blocking points; when a round closes none, stop and decide or defer with
   the open points. A runaway backstop of 4 rounds is logged as a calibration
   signal, not treated as the expected stop.
7. **Outside view.** Before approving a consequential choice, steelman the
   strongest alternative and run a pre-mortem ("it is three months later and
   this failed — why?"). Prefer a cross-model view: the executive shares the
   feature agent's model family and blind spots.
8. **Accountable.** Every decision states why, how confident, and how to undo
   it, in places the owner already looks: the PR body and the epic board.
   When the owner corrects a decision, the correction becomes a line in this
   charter, which every later review reads.
9. **Owner rulings win.** Push back once, with evidence; then comply.
10. **Bias to action.** A deferral costs the owner an interruption. Defer only
    for a one-way door, or low confidence on something user-facing.

Hard rules are unchanged: never merge on its own instruction, never override
a gated verdict, never touch production or shared resources without the
owner, never type into a pipeline's window.

